W("## 1 Introduction\n")
W(prose("intro", "EPI Suite and similar structure-based estimators are the working standard for screening the fate of chemicals. They are least reliable where a designer most needs them: in chemistry that differs from anything measured. A second user is the analytical chemist who detects an unknown compound by high-resolution mass spectrometry. That user has an MS/MS spectrum but no confirmed structure, so a structure-only model cannot be used at all. We treat three input modes as separate first-class cases, each with its own honest answer: **structure only**, **structure + spectrum**, and **spectrum only**.\n\n"
  "Three choices run through the work. (i) Error is always reported as a percentage of a naive guess, so that skill can be told apart from scale. (ii) Results on test compounds that have a close training analogue are reported only *after* results on leak-free compounds. In Run 1, leakage flattered the numbers by a median 15% and went unnoticed until an audit. (iii) Interval coverage is measured on held-out data, never assumed from the nominal level.\n\n"
  "Prior spectrum-to-property models exist. Within the literature we could review (team library; `READER-1/prior_art_spectrum_only.md`), they address single endpoints and use random or chemical-level splits: aquatic toxicity (doi:10.1021/acs.est.2c02536, pp.3-4), fish BCF (doi:10.1021/acsesttox.6c00068, p.D), ready biodegradability (doi:10.1021/acs.analchem.6c02844, pp.4-7) and LC retention (doi:10.1186/s13321-023-00699-8, pp.4-5). We report spectrum-only predictions for a multi-endpoint physicochemical fate set, evaluated on a cluster-disjoint split by distance to training, with conformal intervals and their empirical coverage."))
